# Free key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Without it, clinic websites are not inspected and unverified fields stay "Unknown".
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional. gemini-3.5-flash-lite is the model the agent path was verified
# against, including the thought-signature handling Gemini 3.x requires.
#
# Budget matters more than model choice here: the agent spends 5-6 calls per
# search where the old extractor spent one, so quota is the first thing to run
# out. Two different 429s mean two different fixes — "free_tier_requests,
# limit: N" is a per-model allowance that resets, while "prepayment credits are
# depleted" is account-level billing that no model change will work around.
# Either way the app degrades to the deterministic pipeline and says so.
#
# Google retires dated ids over time (gemini-2.0-flash and gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# are already gone), so on a 404 list what your key can actually reach:
#   curl -H "x-goog-api-key: $GEMINI_API_KEY" \
#     https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# --- Shared store (optional, but see the warning below) ---------------------
# Three things default to in-memory storage: the search-results cache, the
# website-inspection cache, and the per-IP rate limiter on /api/search. Fine
# for `next dev` or a single `next start` process, but a serverless
# deployment runs more than one instance and doesn't share memory between
# them — so "cached for 24h" AND the rate limit itself both silently stop
# holding the moment there's more than one warm instance. The rate limiter
# doesn't fail loud when this happens — it just quietly lets through more
# requests than the stated limit, one instance's count at a time.
#
# Set both of these (a free Upstash Redis database — https://upstash.com —
# works) and createCache.ts and createRateLimiter.ts both switch to it
# automatically; leave either unset and everything falls back to in-memory
# storage with no other change needed. Check which one is currently active
# via GET /api/health.
#
# If you're deploying this to Vercel (or anywhere else that can run more than
# one instance) for anything beyond a demo, set these first.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# --- Accounts (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# Auth.js (next-auth v5) with OAuth only, and no database: the session is a
# signed, encrypted cookie and nothing is stored server-side. Leave all of
# this unset and the app behaves exactly as it did before accounts existed —
# the sign-in link doesn't render, and every visitor is anonymous. Anonymous
# is a supported tier, not a degraded one.
#
# Check whether a deployment can actually sign anyone in via GET /api/health
# ("authConfigured", plus "authProviders" listing the usable ones).
#
# One gotcha: static-vs-dynamic rendering of the home page is decided at build
# time, so these have to be set for the build, not only at runtime, or the
# sign-in link never renders even though /api/health says it is configured.
# On Vercel that is automatic; elsewhere it may not be.

# Required before anyone can sign in. Signs and encrypts the session cookie —
# rotating it logs everyone out, which is the intended emergency lever.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=

# Set the pair for whichever providers you want; either one alone is fine, and
# a provider missing half its pair is skipped rather than offered as a button
# that errors. The variable names are Auth.js's own inference convention.
#
# GitHub: https://github.com/settings/developers -> New OAuth App
#   Authorization callback URL: https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/github
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=

# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials -> OAuth client ID
#   Authorised redirect URI: https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/google
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# Only needed off Vercel. Auth.js trusts the request host automatically on
# Vercel, on Cloudflare Pages, and outside production; anywhere else it needs
# to be told its own public URL, or sign-in redirects come back to the wrong
# origin. Include the scheme, no trailing slash.
# AUTH_URL=https://your-domain.example

# --- Offline / fixture mode (development only) ------------------------------
# Replaces all five upstreams — geocoder, clinic directory, clinic websites,
# and both Gemini calls — with canned test data, so the whole app including
# the agent loop runs with no API key, no quota spent, and no load on the
# volunteer-run OpenStreetMap services.
#
#   USE_FIXTURES=1 npm run dev
#
# NOTHING IT SHOWS IS REAL. It is not blocked in production builds, because
# testing a production build offline is a legitimate need — so never set this
# on a deployment real people can reach. When it is on, the app says so in a
# banner on every page, on the first line of the run's step log, at
# GET /api/health ("upstreams": "fixtures"), and in a startup log warning.
# USE_FIXTURES=1
